1
00:06:11,538 --> 00:06:17,538
Enter the Void is a fantasy, drama film directed by Gaspar Noé.

2
00:12:23,076 --> 00:12:29,076
This psychedelic tour of life after death is seen entirely from the point of view of Oscar, a young
American drug dealer and addict living in Tokyo with his prostitute sister, Linda.

3
00:18:34,615 --> 00:18:40,615
When Oscar is killed by police during a bust gone bad, his spirit journeys from the past -- where he sees his parents before their deaths -- to the
present -- where he witnesses his own autopsy -- and then to the future, where he looks out for his sister from beyond the grave.

4
00:24:46,153 --> 00:24:52,153
Linda, played by Paz de la Huerta.

5
00:30:57,692 --> 00:31:03,692
Oscar, played by Nathaniel Brown.

6
00:37:09,230 --> 00:37:15,230
Alex, played by Cyril Roy.

7
00:43:20,769 --> 00:43:26,769
Victor, played by Olly Alexander.

8
00:49:32,307 --> 00:49:38,307
Mario, played by Masato Tanno.

9
00:55:43,846 --> 00:55:49,846
Bruno, played by Ed Spear.

10
01:01:55,384 --> 01:02:01,384
Enter the Void is a 2009 surrealist art film written and directed by Gaspar Noé,
from a screenplay co-written with his wife Lucile Hadzihalilovic.

11
01:08:06,923 --> 01:08:12,923
Set in the neon-lit nightclub environments of Tokyo, the story follows Oscar (Nathaniel Brown), a young American drug dealer
who gets fatally shot by the police, but continues to watch subsequent events during an out-of-body experience.

12
01:14:18,461 --> 01:14:24,461
The film is shot from a first-person viewpoint with extensive uses of long take, which often floats above the city
streets, and occasionally features Oscar staring over his own shoulder as he recalls moments from his past.

13
01:20:30,000 --> 01:20:36,000
Noé labels the film a "psychedelic melodrama".

14
01:26:41,538 --> 01:26:47,538
A passion project for Noé since his adolescence, the film was in development hell for years until its
production was made possible after the commercial success of his earlier feature, Irréversible (2002).

15
01:32:53,076 --> 01:32:59,076
Enter the Void was primarily financed by Wild Bunch, while
Fidélité Films led the actual production.

16
01:39:04,615 --> 01:39:10,615
With a mix of professionals and newcomers, the film makes heavy use of imagery
inspired by experimental cinema and psychedelic drug experiences.

17
01:45:16,153 --> 01:45:22,153
Principal photography took place on location in Tokyo,
and involved many complicated crane shots.

18
01:51:27,692 --> 01:51:33,692
Co-producers included the visual effects studio BUF Compagnie,
which also provided the computer-generated imagery.

19
01:57:39,230 --> 01:57:45,230
The film's soundtrack is a collage of electronic pop and experimental music.

20
02:03:50,769 --> 02:03:56,769
A rough cut premiered at the 2009 Cannes Film Festival, but post-production work
continued, and the film was not released in France until almost a year later.

21
02:10:02,307 --> 02:10:08,307
A cut-down version was released in the United States
and United Kingdom in September 2010.

22
02:16:13,846 --> 02:16:19,846
The critical response was sharply divided: positive reviews described the film as
captivating and innovative, while negative reviews called it tedious and puerile.

23
02:22:25,384 --> 02:22:31,384
The film performed poorly at the box office.

24
02:28:36,923 --> 02:28:42,923
The film explores themes of japan, prostitute,
afterlife, hallucination, surrealism.

25
02:34:48,461 --> 02:34:54,461
The tone is dark and emotional and mind_bending.
